                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f88afc09-8ee4-3164-aa94-6ce426e199f1",
                        "entry_id": "66e05d08-2b8a-3dbb-909e-009c47c529c9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Growing or living along the banks of rivers"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "dated",
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2ae78ed8-9326-41ce-862a-59d74f7158a0",
                                "sense_id": "f88afc09-8ee4-3164-aa94-6ce426e199f1",
                                "text": "Of these, 18 (or 3 per cent.) are aquatic, 84 (or 14 per cent.) riparious or marshy, 65 ( or 11 per cent.) annuals in fields, of which 22 are found in the spring crops or \"rubby.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c073e75f-2773-4e02-b83d-684197221ae7",
                                "sense_id": "f88afc09-8ee4-3164-aa94-6ce426e199f1",
                                "text": "Hence, there are no real differences between the riparious carabid beetles of Central Africa and the Sahel zone with regard to their annual rhythms and control."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0b4d13f3-a3da-4fe7-9844-9ffbfe5c4869",
                                "sense_id": "f88afc09-8ee4-3164-aa94-6ce426e199f1",
                                "text": "Beside these , other aquatic and riparious beetles, such as members of the families Dytiscidae and Gyrinidae, never have been colonized by nematodes."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9f373e51-aadb-3fa7-af5d-e22ea3b51107",
                        "entry_id": "66e05d08-2b8a-3dbb-909e-009c47c529c9",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Pertaining to riverfront property."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Louisiana",
                            "archaic",
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "9984bf3b-221c-4054-acc4-844787014fc6",
                                "sense_id": "9f373e51-aadb-3fa7-af5d-e22ea3b51107",
                                "text": "It was Settled at that period, that alluvions must belong to the riparious owner , according to this natural maxim, that the profit belongs to him who is exposed to suffer the damage, with which riparious property is threatened, no more than any other."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fbe13be4-4029-4441-8020-af10fa743372",
                                "sense_id": "9f373e51-aadb-3fa7-af5d-e22ea3b51107",
                                "text": "The purchaser of a riparious estate under the words \"front to the levee,\" does not acquire the alluvion or batture, when there is land susceptible of separate ownership beyond the levee."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "221f9c40-0f48-4d4f-8058-ed55de9dd684",
                                "sense_id": "9f373e51-aadb-3fa7-af5d-e22ea3b51107",
                                "text": "First, that the city of New Orleans acquired, at the time of the union of the suburb Delord with the city, all the riparious rights which Madame Delord possessed by virtue of a valid contract, and for a valuable consideration; and secondly, that Madame Delord has never disposed of any portion of her riparious rights in favor of the defendants."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
